0248: Publish a replacement texture within the frame budget, not the whole mip chain at once

Replacement textures (texture packs) are uploaded on the presenting thread: gx::texture::end_frame
runs after gx::fifo::drain and before gfx::finish in aurora::end_frame (lib/aurora.cpp, activity
scope "Host"), and calls texture_replacement::process_streaming. process_streaming took a decoded
replacement and published it whole in that frame: create_converted_texture_handle does
Device::CreateTexture plus write_texture of every mip level, and publish_fits_budget exempted the
frame's first publish from ReplacementPublishBudgetBytes (12 MiB), whatever its size.

A 4096x4096 pack texture is about 21 MB as BC7 with its mips and 85 MB as RGBA8. Upstream that
becomes, on the thread that presents the frame: a memcpy of the whole chain into the frame's upload
range (TextureUploadSize, 24 MiB, lib/gfx/resources.hpp) or, past it, a dedicated mappedAtCreation
"Overflow Texture Upload Buffer" per mip (lib/gfx/recording.cpp, queue_texture_upload_data), the
CopyBufferToTexture commands, and the new texture object. LM's pack playtest
A 30 minute playtest log shows what that costs: worst frame 2911, 3013, 2174 and
1787 ms in the windows at 886-897 s and 95 s with the pack on, none in the runs without it, in
rooms whose textures are that size.

Now the budget is spent strictly, a row range per mip level at a time (write_texture_rows: the same
per-mip layout and upload path write_texture uses, with origin.y at the first block row), and a
publication continues on the next frame until its last range is written. The texture object is
created with the first range and the handle joins the replacement cache only after the last one, so
no draw ever samples a partly written texture: until then the game's own texture is drawn, exactly
as while the replacement file was still decoding. One frame's publishing therefore costs at most
ReplacementPublishBudgetBytes (12 MiB, a row at most 64 KiB) plus one CreateTexture; the multi-second
freeze becomes the original drawn for the frames the chain still needs: two for the 4K BC7 case,
seven for the 85 MB RGBA8 one (0.07 s and 0.23 s at 30 fps).

What is rendered once a replacement is published, the replacement cache and its LRU budget
(set_replacement_cache_budget), the order replacements are chosen in, and the one
invalidate_replacement + invalidate_bindings per completed replacement are unchanged: the same
pixels, and the same cache behaviour, only the upload is spread over frames.
texture_publish_stats() (TexturePublishStats) reports what proves that on the next playtest
(replacements completed, bytes published, frames that ended with work left, the worst single frame
and its time on the presenting thread, the queue behind the budget, bytes in flight, evictions); the
port logs it as the [texpack] line behind LM_TEXPACK_LOG (docs/TEXTURE_PACKS.md).

